INSERT INTO lr_explanation_steps (explanation_id, position, label, body) SELECT e.id, v.position, v.label, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES (0, $pilot$Conclusion (so)$pilot$, $pilot$Some of O'Brien's students must have heard the lecture from the beginning.$pilot$), (1, $pilot$Evidence$pilot$, $pilot$- Any of O'Brien's students who heard it from the beginning found it fascinating.
- Some of O'Brien's students found it fascinating.$pilot$), (2, $pilot$Evaluate$pilot$, $pilot$Whenever we're doing Flaw and we see a conditional premise ("any ... would have"), we go on red alert for the #1 Famous Flaw: Necessary vs. Sufficient.

Here's the rule:
```
O'Brien student +      →   found it
heard from beginning        fascinating
```

An illegal reversal would sound like: "Some students found it fascinating. So they heard it from the beginning." That's exactly what O'Brien did. She saw the outcome (fascinated) and concluded the trigger (heard it from the start).

Conversationally: "Anyone who eats a whole pizza is full. Some of my students are full. So some of them ate a whole pizza." Maybe they had a salad. Being full has other causes. Same here: a student could walk in twenty minutes late and still think it was the best lecture of their life. Nothing in the rule stops latecomers from being fascinated.$pilot$), (3, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$Read the stem carefully: we want a *possibility* that shows the conclusion doesn't follow. That means a world where both premises stay true and the conclusion is false.

The conclusion is false if *no* O'Brien student heard it from the beginning. Picture it: all of her fascinated students came in late. The rule never triggers, some students were fascinated, and zero heard the start.

So look for an answer that builds that world: no O'Brien student heard the lecture from the beginning (or the fascinated ones didn't).$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT159-S1-Q21$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lr_explanation_choice_notes (explanation_id, letter, body) SELECT e.id, v.letter, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES ($pilot$A$pilot$, $pilot$This is the most popular wrong answer. It tells us about students who didn't attend at all. Fine, but the conclusion only needs *some* students to have heard it from the beginning. Students who stayed home say nothing about the ones who came. With (A) true, the fascinated students could still be early arrivals, and the conclusion could still be true.

On "does not follow because of the possibility that" questions, ask of each answer: if this were true, would the conclusion be in trouble? Here, no.$pilot$), ($pilot$B$pilot$, $pilot$The rule only covers O'Brien's students. What other people thought changes neither premise and doesn't touch the conclusion.$pilot$), ($pilot$C$pilot$, $pilot$Late students who weren't fascinated fit perfectly into O'Brien's world. They don't trigger the rule, and they aren't the fascinated students her conclusion is built on. To do any damage, (C) would need the late students to *be* fascinated. It says the opposite.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$It's built to look convoluted, so slow down: nobody who both heard it from the beginning *and* found it fascinating was one of O'Brien's students.

Now bring in the rule. Any O'Brien student who heard it from the beginning *would* have found it fascinating. So if an O'Brien student had heard it from the beginning, that student would be exactly the kind of person (D) says doesn't exist. The only way (D) and the rule can both be true is if no O'Brien student heard it from the beginning.

That makes the conclusion false while both premises still stand: the fascinated students must have come in late. That's the world we predicted.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$Disruptions affect who heard the *entire* lecture. The argument is about who heard the *beginning*. Someone who arrived on time and then got distracted by latecomers still heard the start. (E) doesn't stop any O'Brien student from hearing the beginning, so the conclusion survives.$pi